    "q": "<img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/arp-apq-m4-013-q-1.png\" alt=\"Question 13\n\nShare-based payments - Madison Groff Ltd\n\nMadison Groff Ltd (Madison) prepares its financial statements to 31 December each year, in\naccordance with IFRS Accounting Standards. Madison is considering two possible employee incentive\nschemes. Madison currently has 1,000 employees and approximately 45 employees leave each year.\n\nYou have been asked to compare the following aspects of the two schemes:\n\n    •  Type of scheme\n    •   Accounting treatment\n    •   Valuation basis\n    •   Revision of estimates\n\nThe scheme chosen would commence on 1 January 20X1 and run for 2 years. You have also been\nasked to calculate the cost of each scheme for both years.\n\nScheme 1\n\nEmployees who are still employed by Madison at the end of the 2-year period will each receive 50 share\noptions.\n\nYou are to assume that the fair value of a share option on 1 January 20X1 will be £1.60.\n\nScheme 2\n\nEmployees who are still employed by Madison at the end of the 2-year period will each receive 25 share\nappreciation rights (SAR).\n\nYou are to assume that the fair value of a SAR will be:\n\n    •  £2 on 31 December 20X1\n    •   £4.50 on 31 December 20X2\n\nRequired:\n\n1.  Compare the two employee incentive schemes using the headings provided.\n\n                                                                                    (15 marks)\n\n2.  Calculate, showing all workings, the cost of each scheme for both years.\n\n                                                                                            (8 marks)\n\n                                                                           Total marks (23 marks)\n\n\n\n\n\n14\">",
    "a": "<img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/arp-apq-m4-013-a-1.png\" alt=\"Question 13: Solution\n\nMadison Groff Ltd\n\nComparison\n\n Aspect                  Scheme 1                   Scheme 2\n Type of scheme and accounting  This is an example of an equity-      This is an example of a cash- settled\n treatment                          settled share-based payment        share-based payment scheme.\n                            scheme.                            This scheme will also record an\n                           The scheme must be accounted for   expense, but a liability will be\n                             as an expense (SPL - staff costs),    created rather than a credit to equity.\n                                   with a corresponding credit to equity.  (2 marks)\n                                  (2 marks)\n\n Valuation basis              The fair value of the service given by The cost will be measured based on\n                                 the eligible staff cannot be            the value of the SAR, which is\n                             measured.                        expected to change over the 2- year\n                                 Therefore, the cost will be measured  term of the scheme.\n                                using the fair value of the share     The SAR will be measured using an\n                                 options at 1 January 20X1, the grant  option-pricing model.\n                                   date.                                  Similar to Scheme 1, the vesting\n                           The options should be valued at      period will be considered and a\n                              market price or by using an           proportion of cost recognised each\n                                 appropriate valuation technique (e.g.  year. As this is a 2-year scheme,\n                                 the option- pricing model).             half of the cost will be recognised in\n                           The scheme vests over a 2-year      year 1 and the balance of the full\n                                   period. The scheme cost will be       cost will be recognised in year 2. (3\n                             assessed at the end of each        marks)\n                                accounting period and half of the\n                                  cost will be recognised in the first\n                                   year, with the balance of the full cost\n                               recognised in the second year. (4\n                            marks)\n\n Revision of estimates                    If there are changes to the number   The liability should be adjusted to\n                                     of eligible employees over the          reflect any changes in both the\n                               course of the vesting period, then    number of eligible employees and\n                                 the amount in equity should be       the fair value of the SARs. (2 marks)\n                                 adjusted to take account of the\n                                 revised number of options to be\n                                  issued.\n                             There should be no adjustment for\n                            changes in the fair value of the\n                                 options as the amount should be\n                            based on the fair value at the vesting\n                                   date.\n                                  (2 marks)\n\n\n\n\n\n22\"><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/arp-apq-m4-013-a-2.png\" alt=\"Cost\n\nScheme 1\n\nYear ended        Calculation                                    Expense     Equity\n                                                                      £         £\n31 December 20X1  50 options x (1,000 - 90) employees x £1.60 x 1/2 years      36,400     36,400\n31 December 20X2  (50 options x [1,000 - 90] employees x £1.60) - £36,400       36,400     72,800\n\n                                                                                            (4 marks)\n\nScheme 2\n\nYear ended        Calculation                                     Expense     Liability\n                                                                       £         £\n31 December 20X1  (1,000 - 90) employees x 25 SAR x £2 x 1/2 years           22,750     22,750\n31 December 20X2  ([1,000 - 90] employees x 25 SAR x £4.50) - £22,750         79,625    102,375\n\n                                                                                            (4 marks)\n\n                                                                           Total marks (23 marks)\n\n\n\n\n\n                                                                               23\">",
