    "q": "<img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/arp-apq-m4-016-q-1.png\" alt=\"Question 16\n\nShare-based payments - Sorrento Azure plc\n\nYou are the financial controller of Sorrento Azure plc (Sorrento), a retailer with outlets throughout the\nUK. Sorrento prepares its financial statements in accordance with IFRS Accounting Standards.\n\nThe directors have asked you to analyse the impact of certain share-based payment matters on\nSorrento’s financial statements for the following year ending 31 May 20X4. Relevant information is in\nthe Appendix.\n\nRequired:\n\nAdvise how the items in the Appendix should be accounted for in the year ending 31 May 20X4.\n\n                                                                                    (12 marks)\">",
    "a": "<img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/arp-apq-m4-016-a-1.png\" alt=\"Question 16: Solution\n\nSorrento Azure plc\n\nManagers’ incentive scheme\n\nThis is a cash-settled, share-based payment scheme, with those managers who are still with the\ncompany at the end of the 4-year vesting period on 31 May 20X7 receiving a cash payment.\n\n                                                                                             (1 mark)\n\nThe cost, and an associated liability, will be spread over the vesting period based on an estimate of the\nnumber expected to qualify for the reward and the fair value of the share appreciation rights. As no staff\ncan join after 1 June 20X3, no account is taken of managers recruited after that date.\n\n                                                                                             (1 mark)\n\nThe cost, recorded in the statement of profit or loss for the year ending 31 May 20X4 as staff costs, will\nbe: ((160 - (4 x 8)) x 4,000 x £3.60) / 4 = £460,800\n\n                                                                                            (3 marks)\n\nThe corresponding liability, as not due to be settled until 20X7, will be presented in non-current liabilities\nin the statement of financial position at 31 May 20X4.\n\n                                                                                             (1 mark)\n\nThe liability gives rise to a deductible temporary difference which gives rise to a deferred tax asset of\n£92,160 (£460,800 x 20%).\n\n                                                                                             (1 mark)\n\nThis would reduce the tax expense in the statement of profit or loss.\n\n                                                                                             (1 mark)\n\nNew retail outlets\n\na)  The cash purchase would result in an increase to property, plant and equipment (PPE) of £23m\n    with a corresponding reduction in the bank account balance.\n\n                                                                                             (1 mark)\n\nb)  The purchase in exchange for shares would be based on the fair value of the stores acquired (ie\n   £23m), and not the fair value of the shares given in exchange.\n\n                                                                                             (1 mark)\n\nc) PPE would increase by £23m and equity by the same amount, being share capital of £2m (£4m x\n    50p) and the balance of £21m would be added to share premium.\n\n                                                                                             (1 mark)\n\nIn both cases, the retail outlets would be depreciated from the date of purchase, leading to an expense\nin the statement of profit or loss and a reduction in the carrying amount in the statement of financial\nposition. There is no deferred tax impact relating to the retail outlets.\n\n                                                                                             (1 mark)\n\n                                                                           Total marks (12 marks)\n\n\n\n26\">",
    "isHTML": true,
    "linkTags": [
      "cash-settled-share-based-payments"
    ],
